I’ll inspect the diffraction data, metadata, and the reusable analysis helpers first, then establish an indexed/identified phase inventory before refining any structural model. I’ll preserve stable GSAS-II branches and validate the final project and exports against the supplied audits.

The chemistry is constrained to Sr–W–O from a 900 °C SrCO₃/WO₃ reaction, but both phase count and product stoichiometry remain open. The raw scan uses Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ and has no supplied uncertainties or calibrated profile, so I’ll treat counting-statistical weights and instrument/profile terms cautiously and let complete-pattern evidence decide among likely tungstates and residual reactants.

The peak list already shows that a single scheelite SrWO₄ pattern is unlikely to explain the scan: several strong lines (notably near 33.39°, 45.47°, 51.98°, and 56.08°) form a second coherent family rather than isolated noise. I’m now screening all accessible Sr–W–O and precursor-compatible crystal models by calculated full-pattern fingerprints, including polymorphs as separate hypotheses.

The dominant phase is now securely identified as scheelite SrWO₄ (I4₁/a): its calculated model accounts for the full sequence from 18.33° through 95.38°, not just the strongest peak. A second family—the 23.46/23.97/24.73° triplet plus matching lines near 34.09°, 42.18°, 44.67°, and 48.61°—supports room-temperature distorted WO₃. I rejected one nominal monoclinic WO₃ CIF because its audit exposed 1.57 Å O–O contacts; the chemically sound P2₁/c and P-1 models will be compared on identical refinement settings.

The first screening pass exposed a setup issue rather than a scientific one: GSAS-II’s simulated-histogram constructor left the overall sample scale refinable, making it exactly redundant with phase scale. I’m correcting that before interpreting fractions. Even with that artifact, Sr₃W₂O₉ immediately collapsed to zero scale and destabilized when its cell was released, so it currently fails the required positive-scale/multi-peak test.

The residual is predominantly angular, not a missing phase: SrWO₄ peaks are calculated too low in 2θ at low angle but too high at high angle (for example +0.15° at 28° and −0.14° at 56°). That sign reversal is the fingerprint of specimen-displacement/zero geometry coupled with cell scale. Per the required workflow, I’m branching zero-only, displacement-only, and joint angular models from stable copies before changing widths or adding phases.

Both angular branches are converging slowly because releasing the previously biased cells causes large, coupled corrections, but they are numerically nonsingular. The key result so far is material: correcting angular geometry drops the two-phase fit from about 33.5% Rwp into the low-20% range before any texture, size/strain, or coordinate refinement. I’m letting the prescribed matched branches finish so the selected geometry is reproducible rather than choosing from an intermediate cycle.

The mature residual separates into two coherent components. The 23.46/23.97/24.73° triplet remains consistent with distorted WO₃, while the much stronger 29.76°, 32.62°, 34.50°, 44.67°, 48.61°, 50.22/50.56°, 56.996°, and 61.28° family matches the independent monoclinic SrWO₄ polymorph across the pattern. I’m auditing and adding that atomistic model as a third phase; this avoids incorrectly forcing its intensity into WO₃ or profile broadening.

The proposed monoclinic SrWO₄ candidate failed the full-pattern scale test: at its database cell, its strongest predicted doublet would appear near 28.8–28.9°, where no independent peak exists, so its scale collapsed to zero. The residual instead follows an F-centered cubic sequence (approximately 20.68, 29.76, 35.31/36.72, 42.19, 53.44, 61.28, 70.77, 78.60, 83.91, 93.32°), consistent with a double-perovskite-type cell near 8.5 Å. I’m using that indexed metric only as retrieval evidence to find an atomistic Sr–W–O topology, then I’ll require the complete intensity pattern to pass.

The residual indexing now points to an ordered cubic Sr₃WO₆-type phase rather than the monoclinic SrWO₄ polymorph. I’m building and auditing that charge-balanced atomistic model, then I’ll rerun the complete three-phase comparison—including both near-tied WO₃ structures—from matched refinements.

The cubic model passes the symmetry/occupancy expansion check as Sr₃WO₆ (Fm‑3m). I adjusted its sole oxygen coordinate to give chemically credible W–O and octahedral Sr–O distances before refinement; the phase test will decide whether its scale is supported across the full indexed reflection family.

The third phase retains a positive scale and multi-peak support, but the no-offset baseline forces all three cells away from credible metrics and broadens the global Gaussian term. The P2₁/c WO₃ branch is the only one that converges reproducibly; the triclinic alternative oscillates and distorts its cell, so I’m carrying P2₁/c into the required angular-geometry branch test now.

The required geometry helper is finding a large, real improvement when one angular correction is introduced, but its full-model restoration is still oscillatory. I’m letting all zero-only, displacement-only, and joint branches complete so the selection is based on stability and correlation—not the lower Rwp alone.

With the cells held at their established baseline values, the helper now gives a valid selection: a single specimen-displacement correction of −131 µm. The zero-only branch is worse, and the joint branch is rejected at 99.87% correlation. I’ll now propagate only that selected project and re-establish each cell sequentially before a matched all-cell convergence.

The post-cell rerun again selects displacement, now −244 µm; the zero-only option remains valid but fits the full angular range worse, and the joint option remains unusable. The shift and cells are still moving in a coupled direction, so I’m alternating fixed-shift cell convergence with the mandated helper until that coordinate-descent loop stops changing materially.

At −345 µm the scheelite cell no longer meets the strict 0.05 shift/esd threshold within eight refinement rounds, although every round improves and remains nonsingular. I’m extending that same staged branch—without accepting its intermediate state—until it either converges or is rolled back.

The coupled all-cell test is closer but still unstable once the global width is restored, confirming that simultaneous geometry/cell refinement is over-correlated. I’m retaining the smaller staged model; the helper’s fixed-cell comparisons remain stable and are the appropriate discriminator here.

The latest selected geometry project is stable with displacement −424 µm and a 24.27% Rwp before profile/microstructure optimization. I’m pausing further geometry iteration to perform the required mature-baseline model tests: leave-one-out support for each retained phase, the near-tied triclinic WO₃ replacement, both previously rejected tungstates, and the unreacted SrCO₃ precursor.

I caught and contained a GSAS-II scripting side effect: importing a phase auto-saved into the loaded scratch project, so later candidate tests inherited an earlier replacement. No submitted file was affected. I stopped that run, restored the clean three-phase baseline from the helper’s selected branch, and am rerunning each candidate in an isolated project copy.

The mature model tests are decisive: removing Sr₃WO₆ worsens Rwp by 0.49 points and removing WO₃ by 1.09; both are supported independently. Monoclinic SrWO₄ and SrCO₃ collapse to zero scale. Sr₃W₂O₉ is only 0.33(31) wt% with negligible benefit and its cell branch becomes invalid. Triclinic WO₃ is worse at fixed metric and its nominal 0.02-point gain appears only in a nonconvergent six-cell-parameter branch, so P2₁/c is retained.

The phase inventory is now stable: SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and a minor Fm‑3m Sr₃WO₆ model all have independent multi-peak support, while SrCO₃ and the tested alternative tungstates collapse or add negligible benefit. I’m moving to matched profile/envelope branches from the converged three-phase baseline, with cells and specimen displacement held fixed during each comparison.

The residual map shows two distinct issues to separate: peak centroids still drift slightly (especially around the intense 28° doublet), while the strongest SrWO₄ families have alternating intensity errors across the full range. I’ll first test the smallest angle-dependent Gaussian and Lorentzian width terms independently; intensity-envelope and crystallite terms will be tested only after that baseline is settled.

The constant-width control reconverged unchanged. Allowing an additional Gaussian angle term is already showing unstable back-and-forth steps rather than a reproducible improvement; I’m letting the isolated branches finish so that rejection is based on their full-pattern behavior and covariance, not a single iteration.

The envelope comparison is revealing but not acceptable as a retained correction: polarization drives to its physical upper bound, and the roughness branch is already >95% correlated with the dominant-phase scale. Those are precisely the boundary/correlation failures the requested integrity checks call for; I’ll preserve the branch metrics, then return to the simpler envelope.

A setup issue surfaced in the phase-broadening screen: GSAS-II’s newly linked phases carry nonzero fixed defaults (1 µm size and 1000 µstrain), so “unrefined” was not actually a zero-broadening control. I’m discarding this screen, resetting all phases to effectively infinite size and zero strain, reconverging W, and then repeating each phase term from that true null baseline.

With the true null control, only SrWO₄ crystallite size shows a reproducible positive solution so far (about 0.26 µm, ΔRwp ≈ 0.07 percentage point); WO₃ size is effectively infinite and both tested microstrain terms return to zero. I’m finishing the minor cubic-phase tests before deciding whether the modest SrWO₄ term survives reconvergence and leave-one-term removal.

Both retained broadening terms pass matched removal tests: dropping SrWO₄ size raises Rwp from 21.811% to 21.905%, and dropping Sr₃WO₆ microstrain raises it to 21.941%. No other phase width term survives. I’m now rerunning the required displacement/zero comparison from this updated profile state before any intensity-model tests.

The post-profile angular helper again selects displacement-only (now −528 µm); the joint zero/shift branch is rejected at 99.86% correlation despite its lower numerical Rwp. Cell restaging is converging around that fixed displacement and has already reduced the complete-pattern Rwp to about 20.73%.

The texture screen supports at most a weak SrWO₄ [100] March–Dollase term (Rwp 20.744→20.678, ratio 1.143). Other SrWO₄/WO₃ axes return essentially unity. The cubic [111] branch is numerically divergent, so I’m stopping that failed branch and will test the cubic [100] axis separately before deciding on the weak SrWO₄ correction.

The only usable texture candidate is the weak SrWO₄ [100] term; cubic texture branches fail numerically, and all other ratios are effectively unity. I’m holding [100] provisional while testing the few symmetry-independent coordinates that can materially change intensities—scheelite O, cubic-perovskite O, and the two WO₃ W sites—then I’ll retest texture from the better structural baseline.

I’m finishing the structure-parameter branch checks now. The key decision is whether their apparent fit gains survive chemical-geometry audits; after that I’ll lock the smallest stable model, rerun the angular/profile checks, and export the audited deliverables.

The coordinate branches are now chemically resolved: the tempting SrWO₄ and Sr₃WO₆ gains require W–O distances of about 2.10 Å and 1.39 Å, respectively, and the WO₃ branch gives only a tiny gain with strongly correlated, large W shifts and a 1.55 Å contact. I’m rejecting all three and retaining the audited source geometries. The texture tests were likewise too small or unstable to justify another parameter, so the final model remains deliberately minimal.

The required angular recheck confirms the baseline is the replay-safe choice: zero-only, shift-only, and joint branches all lower the nominal Rwp only by entering nonconverged solutions (maximum shift/σ 4.1–5.7), while zero+shift remains 99.85% correlated. I’m retaining the stable displacement-only solution at −527.9 µm with fixed refined cells (Rwp 20.744%, max shift/σ 0.040).

One retest harness exposed a GSAS-II scripting quirk: adding a phase auto-saved into the loaded project before the branch filename changed. I stopped that run immediately; the stable pre-branch project is preserved in the angular-check copy, and I’m restoring from that copy before continuing. No submitted output has been created yet.

The mature leave-one-out tests remain decisive on the updated model: removing SrWO₄ raises Rwp from 20.744% to 41.442%, removing WO₃ to 22.169%, and removing Sr₃WO₆ to 21.408%. The WO₃ triclinic replacement is being carried through its fixed/free-cell checks; its fixed-cell fit is already worse than the retained monoclinic model.

Both retained broadening terms pass the final matched-removal test. Removing SrWO₄ crystallite size worsens Rwp by 0.105 percentage point; removing Sr₃WO₆ microstrain worsens it by about 0.15 point and destabilizes convergence. I’m now freezing the three-phase baseline, regenerating its profile/reflections, and auditing the exact exported state.

Completed the three-phase refinement.

- SrWO₄ (I4₁/a): 81.64(52) wt%
- WO₃ (P2₁/c model): 9.84(28) wt%
- Sr₃WO₆ (tentative Fm-3m model): 8.52(48) wt%

Rwp = 20.744%, Rp = 15.194%, GOF = 5.877. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. The Sr₃WO₆ identity/fraction remains semi-quantitative, and the exact WO₃ polymorph is moderately uncertain.

